   "type": "answer",
   "content": "No. Reading VCF Operations CPU|Demand as unmet demand is wrong; the gap is reserved capacity, not contention.\n\nMechanism: ESXi CPU Demand is a sum-of-consumers metric. vCenter `cpu.demand` is the sum of the running VMs’ ideal demand with no contention/limit; it excludes VM CPU reservations and the kernel System Resource Reservation. VCF Operations `CPU|Demand` is the capacity-model demand: per running VM the higher of its demand and its CPU reservation, summed, plus the kernel reservation. Usage is actual use in both tools.\n\nReason: on a quiet host with few VMs, VCF Operations CPU|Demand ≈2.91-2.92 GHz flat while CPU|Usage ≈324-484 MHz. That flat high value is the kernel reservation plus VM reservations, not VM work. vCenter Demand ≈203 MHz avg, 223 MHz max, shows real VM demand ≈0.2 GHz. ESXi CPU Reserved Capacity includes only VM reservations and excludes the kernel reservation; VCF Operations Demand adds the kernel reservation on top of the per-VM max(demand, reservation). Unmet demand is contention, not Demand minus Usage.\n\nCheck next: VM CPU Ready/Co-stop peaks on the host’s VMs, ESXi Core/Thread Utilization, and ESXi CPU Reserved Capacity vs base capacity. Demand above Usage in VCF Operations can be reservation alone.\n\n`vsphere/technology/cpu/metrics/host/demand`; `vsphere/technology/cpu/metrics/host/reserved-capacity`; `vsphere/architecture/provider-consumer`; `sp5-framework/capacity/demand`.",
